{"name":"Butterfly Kisses","hex":"#f0dedc","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"butterfly kisses","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["plural of butterfly kiss"]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/butterfly_kisses"}],"entries":[{"title":"Butterfly Kisses (song)","blocks":[{"text":"\"Butterfly Kisses\" is a song written by Bob Carlisle and Randy Thomas from Carlisle's third studio album, Butterfly Kisses . The song was written for Carlisle's daughter Brooke's 16th birthday. Carlisle also wrote a journal, Butterfly Kisses for Fathers and Their Daughters. The last track of Butterfly Kisses  is a country version of the song, where instruments like the pedal steel guitar and fiddle are added. There have been many cover versions of the song including Raybon Brothers, Jeff Carson, Westlife and Cliff Richard."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/thumb/7/70/Butterfly_Kisses_single.jpg/330px-Butterfly_Kisses_single.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Butterfly_Kisses_(song)"},{"title":"Butterfly Kisses (Shades of Grace)","blocks":[{"text":"Butterfly Kisses (Shades of Grace) is the third studio album by American Christian music artist Bob Carlisle. It was released by Diadem/Jive Records on May 13, 1997. The album peaked at No. 1 on the Billboard 200."}],"image":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/d/d2/ShadesofGrace.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Butterfly_Kisses_(Shades_of_Grace)"},{"title":"Butterfly","blocks":[{"text":"Butterflies are winged insects from the lepidopteran superfamily Papilionoidea, characterised by large, often brightly coloured wings that often fold together when at rest, and a conspicuous, fluttering flight. The oldest butterfly fossils have been dated to the Paleocene, about 56 million years ago, though molecular evidence suggests that they likely originated in the Cretaceous."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/3d/Fesoj_-_Papilio_machaon_%28by%29.jpg/330px-Fesoj_-_Papilio_machaon_%28by%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Butterfly"},{"title":"Kiss","blocks":[{"text":"A kiss is the touching or pressing of one's lips against another person, animal, or object. The cultural connotations associated with kissing vary significantly between different societies and contexts. Depending on the culture and circumstances, a kiss can express a wide range of emotions, such as love, passion, romance, sexual attraction, sexual arousal, affection, respect, a wish for peace or good luck, as well as numerous other meanings. In some situations, kissing takes on the function of a greeting or a ritual, formal, or symbolic gesture indicating religious devotion, institutional respect, or an act of sacramental nature."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/2b/Cassatt_Mary_Maternal_Kiss_1896.jpg/330px-Cassatt_Mary_Maternal_Kiss_1896.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kiss"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/b/bf/Raybonbutterfly.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled 1x","src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/b/bf/Raybonbutterfly.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ARaybonbutterfly.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/22/Common_brimstone_butterfly_%28Gonepteryx_rhamni%29_male_in_flight.jpg/500px-Common_brimstone_butterfly_%28Gonepteryx_rhamni%29_male_in_flight.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/22/Common_brimstone_butterfly_%28Gonepteryx_rhamni%29_male_in_flight.jpg/1280px-Common_brimstone_butterfly_%28Gonepteryx_rhamni%29_male_in_flight.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/22/Common_brimstone_butterfly_%28Gonepteryx_rhamni%29_male_in_flight.jpg/500px-Common_brimstone_butterfly_%28Gonepteryx_rhamni%29_male_in_flight.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Possibly the original butter-fly.[7] A male brimstone (Gonepteryx rhamni) in flight.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ACommon_brimstone_butterfly_(Gonepteryx_rhamni)_male_in_flight.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/bc/Prodryas.png/500px-Prodryas.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/b/bc/Prodryas.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/bc/Prodryas.png/500px-Prodryas.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AProdryas.png"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/0a/PZSL1889Plate31_Fossil_Papilionid_Butterfly_Lithopsyche_antiqua_from_Early_Oligocene_Bembridge_Marls.png/500px-PZSL1889Plate31_Fossil_Papilionid_Butterfly_Lithopsyche_antiqua_from_Early_Oligocene_Bembridge_Marls.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/0a/PZSL1889Plate31_Fossil_Papilionid_Butterfly_Lithopsyche_antiqua_from_Early_Oligocene_Bembridge_Marls.png/960px-PZSL1889Plate31_Fossil_Papilionid_Butterfly_Lithopsyche_antiqua_from_Early_Oligocene_Bembridge_Marls.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wi